{
  "result": "Every morning, the bakery on my street puts a wooden chair outside its door. Nobody sits in it. By noon, the chair has collected a stripe of sunlight, a dusting of flour, and occasionally a pigeon. The baker says it is there for customers who need a rest. I think it is also there to remind the street that resting is allowed.\n\nWe live among invitations to hurry. Phones buzz, buses sigh at curbs, and lists grow longer while we are checking them. Even leisure can become a task, measured in books finished or miles walked. Yet the chair makes no promises. It offers neither improvement nor entertainment, only a place to put down the weight of standing.\n\nOne rainy Tuesday, an elderly woman stopped beside it. She examined the wet seat, opened her umbrella, and sat anyway. For several minutes she watched the traffic with the composure of someone attending a play whose ending she already knew. When she rose, she left a small paper bag on the chair. Inside was a bread roll, still warm.\n\nThe baker found it and laughed. Perhaps she meant it for a friend, he said. Perhaps she meant it for the pigeons. He placed the bag on the windowsill, where it remained until closing. No one claimed it.\n\nI like that the story has no proper ending. A chair appeared, someone rested, and a kindness with an uncertain destination waited by the window. Tomorrow, the chair will be outside again. That seems enough.",
